<div class="page-intro prose">
  <h1>About this site</h1>
  <p class="lede">I built this to explain UK government debt and the public finances, using official data and plain English. It is independent and non-partisan. No government department, political party or campaign stands behind it.</p>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p>{{ site.title }} pulls together the headline measures of the UK public finances (debt, borrowing, and the interest bill) from official sources. The aim is to help you understand the numbers behind the debate, not to argue a position within it. Every figure shows its source and the date it refers to, so you can check any of it against the original.</p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It is not an official government service, and it is not endorsed by one.</li>
    <li>It does not take a view on what the government should do. The interactive tools let you explore trade-offs; they do not recommend any.</li>
    <li>It is not a forecast. Where figures look ahead, that is clearly flagged.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who runs it</h2>
  <p>I am {{ site.author }}, and I build and maintain this on my own as an educational project. No party, think tank or government body funds it or is connected to it. The source code is public on <a href="{{ site.repo }}" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>, so you can read exactly how any figure is calculated and any chart is drawn.</p>
  <p>I also built the <a href="https://ukmigrationexplorer.org" rel="noopener">UK Migration Explorer</a>, which does the same job for UK migration and asylum statistics: what each figure counts, where it comes from, and what it does not show. Same method, different subject.</p>

  <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
  <p>The data is drawn from primary official sources, chiefly the <abbr title="Office for National Statistics">ONS</abbr>, the <abbr title="Office for Budget Responsibility">OBR</abbr>, the UK Debt Management Office, HM Treasury, and the <abbr title="International Monetary Fund">IMF</abbr> for international comparisons. The <a href="{{ '/sources/' | url }}">sources page</a> lists each one and what it covers.</p>
  <p>Every figure carries its provenance in the underlying data: the value, its unit, the date it refers to, the named source, a link, and the date it was retrieved. The headline figures were last reviewed on {{ meta.lastReviewed | readableDate }}.</p>

  <h2>How confident each figure is</h2>
  <p>Every figure carries a label for how solid it is:</p>
  <dl class="def-list">
    {%- for key, desc in meta.confidenceLevels %}
    <dt>{{ key }}</dt>
    <dd>{{ desc }}</dd>
    {%- endfor %}
  </dl>
  <p>{{ meta.footerNote }}</p>

  <h2>The interactive tools are models, not forecasts</h2>
  <p>The budget simulator and the big-numbers translator are teaching models, kept simple on purpose. They apply static, first-round arithmetic and nothing else. Change a tax rate and the model will not tell you how the economy responds, because it ignores growth, inflation and behaviour. Use them to see how big the trade-offs are, not to predict what would happen. Each one lists its assumptions on its own page.</p>

  <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
  <p>Accuracy matters more than anything else here. If you spot an error (a wrong figure, a broken or outdated source link, or a misleading description), tell me. I will fix it and record the change in the project's public history.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Email <a href="mailto:corrections@ukpublicfinances.org?subject=Correction%3A%20UK%20Public%20Finances%20Explorer">corrections@ukpublicfinances.org</a>, ideally with the page and the figure in question.</li>
    <li>Or, if you prefer, open an issue on <a href="{{ site.repo }}/issues" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Because the site is open source, every correction is visible in the <a href="{{ site.repo }}/commits" rel="noopener">commit history</a>. There is no quiet editing.</p>
</div>
